    "font": {
      "sans": {
        "value": "-apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, \"SF Pro Text\", \"SF Pro Display\", \"Inter\", \"Segoe UI\", Roboto, \"Noto Sans Tamil\", \"Noto Sans Telugu\", \"Noto Sans Devanagari\", system-ui, sans-serif",
        "type": "fontFamily",
        "_comment": "P7 L262 family strategy, verbatim. System font FIRST is the load-bearing part: SF Pro is not licensed for web embedding, so Apple devices resolve San Francisco natively with zero bytes shipped, and every other platform falls to its own UI font. Do NOT move \"SF Pro Display\" to the front -- it is the display cut, optically corrected for >=20pt and measurably worse at caption/footnote sizes. The previous value led with it and also named two webfonts (\"Geist\", \"Plus Jakarta Sans\") that no longer exist, so the app silently rendered the DISPLAY cut at every size from 12px up. Tamil/Telugu/Devanagari are load-bearing, not decorative: user-entered task text is expected in those scripts (P7 L262, dir=\"auto\"), and a missing tail entry is tofu."
      },
      "display": {
        "value": "-apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, \"SF Pro Display\", \"SF Pro Text\", \"Inter\", \"Segoe UI\", Roboto, \"Noto Sans Tamil\", \"Noto Sans Telugu\", \"Noto Sans Devanagari\", system-ui, sans-serif",
        "type": "fontFamily",
        "_comment": "Same stack with the two SF cuts swapped, for type.timer / type.largeTitle / type.title1-3 (>=20pt) where the Display cut is the optically correct one. Apple ships SF Pro in two cuts precisely because they differ at these sizes: Display is tighter, Text is more open. Since neither can be embedded, the OS picks by family name, so expressing this as its own token is the only way to say 'display cut where it belongs' without shipping a webfont."
      },
      "mono": {
        "value": "ui-monospace, \"SF Mono\", SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Consolas, \"Cascadia Mono\", monospace",
        "type": "fontFamily",
        "_comment": "P7 L264. \"SF Mono\" is the family name and SFMono-Regular the PostScript name; both are listed because browsers accept either and macOS is inconsistent about which one it exposes to CSS."
      }
